00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:55.020
And I think by the time we ended the series, he was very three-dimensional, but didn't start

514
00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:55.480
that way.

515
00:21:55.980 --> 00:22:00.820
And, you know, I always also felt like, you know, Andy started as the Connecticut Dwight

516
00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:05.180
in season three for Jim when he was in Stanford.

517
00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:05.880
Yeah.

518
00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:09.580
So he had certain, you know, like his anger management probleMs.

519
00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:14.220
Like there were certain things that were built into Andy that also were a little bit, made

520
00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:15.080
it a little bit difficult.

521
00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:20.240
Like we weren't starting fresh with a person who was conceived to be the boss of the ensemble.

522
00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:23.960
But there's talent for days in our caSt.

523
00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:25.620
You're so right, though.

524
00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:29.460
I hadn't thought of it from that point of view, but it's so clear when you say it that

525
00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:35.020
all these characters were exactly where they were supposed to be in sort of the placement

526
00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:35.780
of a story.

527
00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:40.700
Kate Flannery said this thing one time on, we'd gone to some red carpet thing and we were being

528
00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:42.660
interviewed and we were walking the carpet together.

529
00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:43.840
It was early in the show.

530
00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:49.300
And the interviewer said, the question was sort of like, what's it like to be a supporting

531
00:22:49.300 --> 00:22:50.460
character on the show?

532
00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:52.900
You know, is there a storyline that you've been excited about?

533
00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:53.680
Something like that.

534
00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:58.320
And Kate said, well, first of all, she said, I love being a supporting character on the

535
00:22:58.320 --> 00:22:58.620
show.

536
00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:01.780
And she said, you know, Meredith is not the entree.

537
00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:03.560
She's a little bit of seasoning.

538
00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:04.740
She's some spice.

539
00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:08.620
And I love being the seasoning and the spice, but I'm not the entree.

540
00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:13.540
And it always stuck with me because I was standing right next to her and I was like, she had

541
00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:17.680
such a clear view of her character and her role on the show.

542
00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:22.520
I just thought it was just so smart of Kate of how she answered that.

543
00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:24.340
Those questions can be tricky sometimes.

544
00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:26.580
They're thrown to you and you're like, what do I say?

545
00:23:26.860 --> 00:23:31.540
And I never forgot it and I carried it with me the whole entire rest of the show.

546
00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:36.700
I think Angela is the bitter greens on the plate.

547
00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:37.180
Yeah.

548
00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:38.980
I'm not sure what Pam is.

549
00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:42.040
Maybe like a mushroom sauce or something.

550
00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:43.200
Yes.

551
00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:43.920
Potatoes.

552
00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:45.200
No, she's mashed potatoes.

553
00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:46.020
Potatoes.

554
00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:46.220
Yeah.

555
00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:47.320
We both thought potatoes.

556
00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:47.900
Yes.

557
00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:51.460
I thought my first thing before you said potato was mashed potatoes.

558
00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:56.060
I thought that, you know, if Michael is the steak, then Pam is the mushroom sauce that

559
00:23:56.060 --> 00:23:58.840
makes the steak go down even smoother, even better.

560
00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:00.640
But you can have steak without mushroom sauce.

561
00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:01.320
I guess that's true.

562
00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:02.320
You can't have steak without potatoes.

563
00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:02.840
Okay.

564
00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:03.180
Okay.

565
00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:03.980
I'll take it.

566
00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:04.220
Yeah.

567
00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:07.060
Well, we are on the sales call.

568
00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:08.580
We are on the sales call with D'Angelo and Andy.

569
00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:12.280
The client was played by Alfred Rubin Thompson.

570
00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:18.880
I don't know if you noticed, but at 23 minutes and 45 seconds, he has a CD on his desk that

571
00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:20.340
says currency converter.

572
00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:22.380
I loved this detail.

573
00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:24.020
Oh, that's a great detail.

574
00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:26.720
Way to go, Steve Rothstein and our set dressing department.

575
00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:30.720
So this guy does something where he needs to convert currency.

576
00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:35.660
This is a great two-hander storyline for Ed and Will.

577
00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:48.620
And I think in this moment, Will is being very funny saying this horribly undermining speech about, you know, how Andy is as a flawed salesperson.

578
00:24:48.620 --> 00:24:51.680
But Ed is being equally funny reacting to it.

579
00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:57.680
And it's just, it's really a fun scene to see the two of them together.

580
00:24:57.960 --> 00:24:59.340
They're very funny together.

581
00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:01.780
I needed Andy to have a win.

582
00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:06.540
Like, as I was watching this, I was like, please, please don't let the sale, like, get loSt.

583
00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:12.460
And then when he goes back in and he is able to make the sale and they get the call, I was so happy.

584
00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:13.080
Yeah.

585
00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:14.340
Well, it's interesting.

586
00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:26.240
One of the, when I refer back to the episodes in the beginning of season two where we kind of rebuilt Michael, one of the big ones was the client that Paul wrote.

587
00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:26.700
Yeah.

588
00:25:26.840 --> 00:25:32.020
Where he reveals that he may not be a great manager, but he was really good as a salesperson.

589
00:25:32.020 --> 00:25:38.740
And I think maybe we were doing a little bit here with Ed to try to build him up for the long term for season eight.

590
00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:39.340
Yeah.

591
00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:39.600
I recall.

592
00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:48.340
So apparently part of Pam's process for pricing Shredders is going to the movies and she's going to sneak off and see The King's Speech.

593
00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:50.040
It's a really great movie, you guys.

594
00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:50.840
It is a good movie.

595
00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:51.720
I enjoyed it when I saw it.

596
00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:52.580
I really did too.

597
00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:59.260
Also, to me, the fact that Pam would pick a Colin Firth movie to sneak off to is very telling.

598
00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:00.340
It's very Pam.

599
00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:00.780
Yeah.

600
00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:02.660
We shot that in Pomona.

601
00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:06.120
It was the morning that we did the airport scene.

602
00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:13.860
So while they were sort of setting up the airport and building out that set, we sneaked away and we shot on the street.

603
00:26:14.120 --> 00:26:21.740
You can see they did their best to frame out the word Pomona on the big sign above the movie theater.

604
00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:25.240
But you can, if you know that's what it says, that you can still kind of see it.

605
00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:30.940
But I thought that the coolest thing they did was, if you look for it, you can see, it's not a marquee.

606
00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:31.320
What is it?

607
00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:35.300
You know, in the glass box next to the movie theater, we put a poster for Bridesmaids.

608
00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:35.740
Yeah.

609
00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:36.460
Oh, yes.

610
00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:37.920
As a nod to Ellie and Paul.

611
00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:38.380
Yes.

612
00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:43.300
I'm curious, do you guys think that Jim knew that Pam was going to see a movie?

613
00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:45.180
I don't think he knew.

614
00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:46.040
I don't either.

615
00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:46.640
I don't either.

616
00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:48.920
No, this is her thing.

617
00:26:49.100 --> 00:26:49.880
This is her time.

618
00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:51.280
Because it's Colin Firth.

619
00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:57.920
I used to do this at a job that I had as a media buyer.

620
00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.060
You would sneak off and watch movies?

621
00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:07.000
Well, part of our job, because we bought time to advertise movies, part of our job was

622
00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:10.560
that we got to leave to go see screenings of movies.

623
00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:11.600
It was really cool.

624
00:27:12.100 --> 00:27:17.440
But we would, after the movie, my girlfriend and I, who both worked there, around the corner

625
00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:20.660
from the screening area was a place where you get a manicure pedicure.

626
00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:24.920
And we would just tack one on to the end of our day out.

627
00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:26.200
It was kind of fun.

628
00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:30.900
That's the best, is when you have a job where there's an excuse for you to be out of the

629
00:27:30.900 --> 00:27:31.320
office.

630
00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:32.180
Right.

631
00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:35.640
That's slightly fuzzy about when you're going to get back.

632
00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:36.160
Yeah.

633
00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:36.580
Yeah.

634
00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:37.560
Exactly.

635
00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:40.120
I did do this when I interned on Conan.

636
00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:44.780
If they would send us on a run, you would just go sit for a little bit in a coffee shop.

637
00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:45.700
Take your time.

638
00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:46.540
Have a snack.

639
00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:49.200
Enjoy a tea in Central Park.

640
00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:55.960
I was a proofreader in one job, and it was in a very small publishing house.

641
00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:58.760
It was in a guy's house, and he had a three-story house.

642
00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:01.060
And there were two, I had two bosses.

643
00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:03.920
One of them was on the first floor, and one of them was on the third floor.

644
00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:04.340
Okay.

645
00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:06.900
And sometimes I would just go to the second floor.

646
00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:10.560
And they always think you were with the other.

647
00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:10.760
Yeah.

648
00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:14.920
They were like, oh, he must be down on the first floor working on that guy project.

649
00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:19.520
Well, Michael is going to have his very last conference room meeting, Greg.

650
00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:23.900
Did you think a lot about how you wanted him to have a conference room meeting in this episode?

651
00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:27.900
Well, I think he's getting panicked because his time is running out.

652
00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:28.360
Right.

653
00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:31.300
And I think that he enjoyed conference room meetings.

654
00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:31.820
Yeah.

655
00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:35.440
Because he was able to use them as tryouts for his stand-up.

656
00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:37.300
Exactly.

657
00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:38.480
And have a captive audience.

658
00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:39.460
So, yeah.

659
00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:42.300
So he calls them without really knowing what he's going to say, I think.

660
00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:46.660
Which is very on brand, because I feel like Michael called a lot of meetings like that.

661
00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:46.980
Yeah.

662
00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:52.460
I was very sad that I was not in the last conference room meeting.

663
00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:54.240
This was a bummer for me.

664
00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:56.500
These are some of my happiest times on set.

665
00:28:56.840 --> 00:28:57.020
Yeah.

666
00:28:57.520 --> 00:28:58.220
That was rough.

667
00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:03.420
Well, if you go to 28 minutes and three seconds, I know this is what everyone's looking for in this scene.

668
00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:05.120
My hair is back in the clip again.

669
00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:06.020
Oh, good.

670
00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:07.720
The continuity has returned.

671
00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:09.640
That's what struck me the first time when we saw this.

672
00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:10.280
I was like.

673
00:29:10.400 --> 00:29:11.620
What's up with Angela's hair clip?

674
00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:12.500
Angela's hair's back.

675
00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:13.040
Yeah.

676
00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:15.360
All will be right with the world.

677
00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:22.380
Well, in this conference room meeting, Jim is going to figure out that something is going on.

678
00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:25.380
And he asks Michael if he can speak to him in his office.

679
00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:27.880
I mean, Michael's getting a little unhinged.

680
00:29:28.100 --> 00:29:28.420
Yes.

681
00:29:28.640 --> 00:29:30.360
And Jim is the most perceptive.

682
00:29:31.460 --> 00:29:31.720
Yes.

683
00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:34.260
So he is the one who's going to figure this out.

684
00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:37.940
When did you decide that Jim was going to be the person who figures it out?

685
00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:41.120
Well, you know, everybody has their moment with Michael.

686
00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:51.880
Well, and Jim, it just seems like Michael is sort of graduating psychologically into being a wiser character here.

687
00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:55.960
And, you know, as he's going to float above the office into the clouds.

688
00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:56.380
Yes.

689
00:29:57.660 --> 00:30:01.420
And there is like this little ruse going in the middle of the show.

690
00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:08.360
And for Jim to have a moment and not be in on it, it seemed like it was good for his character that he figures it out.

691
00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:12.100
And they can have the only like sincere conversation.

692
00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:15.180
But again, we started to roll on the last day.

693
00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:17.460
And it was the only sincere conversation.

694
00:30:18.160 --> 00:30:20.040
And it got very emotional.

695
00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:20.580
Yeah.

696
00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:26.980
This was the second to last scene that we shot that day was the scene between Michael and Jim and Michael's office.

697
00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:34.220
Well, if you guys have the DVD box set, it's definitely worth watching this scene because it's a bigger scene.

698
00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:42.200
There is this great beat in the middle of it, Greg, which I understand why it was probably deleted just because the flow of the scene is so perfect.

699
00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:44.340
But it's such a great detail.

700
00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:49.560
So Michael is going to share with Jim that he has a job interview already set up in Colorado.

701
00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:55.220
It's for a paper company that makes the ticket stubs for all the movie theaters.

702
00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:57.640
And he says, you know, it's perfect.

703
00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:00.140
It combines both my loves, movies and paper.

704
00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:02.620
And I just thought that was such a great detail.

705
00:31:02.860 --> 00:31:08.480
I like to know in my brain, like, you know, where Michael is, where he's living, what he's up to.

706
00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:12.620
And I just sort of see him working for the ticket stub paper company.

707
00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:13.140
Yeah.

708
00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:18.260
Well, you know, my dad had two great friends growing up, Dick Town and Tom Hazen.

709
00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:19.480
And I can't remember which one.

710
00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:21.760
One of them had a family paper company.

711
00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:25.160
And they transitioned into tickets.

712
00:31:25.480 --> 00:31:29.600
And that saved a lot of small, or at least it really helped.

713
00:31:29.800 --> 00:31:30.920
Small paper companies?

714
00:31:30.920 --> 00:31:33.840
That small paper company because it was a very high-tech form of paper.

715
00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:37.120
It had all these embossed, you know, things in it and everything.

716
00:31:37.320 --> 00:31:41.760
I was probably on somebody's phone now, so it's probably, it was only a temporary reprieve.

717
00:31:41.840 --> 00:31:44.300
But I remember being told that around that time.

718
00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:52.600
And it did seem like a great job for Michael because it was paper, but it was a little show-busy form of paper, you know.

719
00:31:53.580 --> 00:32:05.520
But the thing about that scene is when it got so sort of real and John was saying stuff to the actor, Steve Carell, about how he felt about working with him for seven years,

720
00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:07.940
it started to really spread.

721
00:32:08.140 --> 00:32:11.520
And you were like, God, this can't be an eight-minute scene.

722
00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:15.480
And, you know, so we were throwing lines out, you know, in the editing room.

723
00:32:15.760 --> 00:32:20.300
It was just, it was just down to the sort of the nub version of it.

724
00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:22.660
But it was, it came out well, I think.

725
00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:24.740
And it was emotional to watch them.

726
00:32:25.060 --> 00:32:33.420
It was emotional to rewatch when, you know, Jim says, but we clearly just saw John in the moment saying when he said, you're the best boss I've ever had.

727
00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:35.520
I was just like, forget it.

728
00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:35.860
Yeah.

729
00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:37.200
I'm a mess.

730
00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:39.860
George Clooney and Leatherheads didn't compare.

731
00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:45.220
We had a fan catch from Juliana R.

732
00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:49.340
in Atlanta, Georgia, who said, I have a fan catch.

733
00:32:49.480 --> 00:32:52.840
Michael forgot his voice recorder in his desk on the last day.

734
00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:59.320
You know, in that scene, he gets out his voice recorder because he has a T-shirt idea, goodbye, stink.

735
00:32:59.320 --> 00:32:59.680
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

736
00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:01.280
And then he puts it back in the drawer.

737
00:33:01.420 --> 00:33:02.340
And he left without it.

738
00:33:02.420 --> 00:33:02.760
Yes.

739
00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:03.480
Justin A.

740
00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:07.380
from Buffalo, New York noticed this as well and wanted to know, was this an intentional joke?

741
00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:08.780
Because they both really enjoyed it.

742
00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:10.760
They both enjoyed the fact that he forgot to bring it.

743
00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:11.260
Yes, yes.

744
00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:15.700
That he left his, he walks out without his voice recorder with all his good ideas on it.

745
00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:16.400
Yeah.

746
00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:17.040
Oh, goodness.

747
00:33:17.320 --> 00:33:21.920
I don't think that was intentional, but I'm glad that they're perceiving it as a joke.

748
00:33:22.080 --> 00:33:22.460
Own it.

749
00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:22.940
Yeah.

750
00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:23.520
Yes.

751
00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:27.780
I like the idea that it's a wonderful leave behind for the next boss.

752
00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:28.120
Right.

753
00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:30.920
A drawer full of amazing ideas.

754
00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:33.720
I like that T-shirt, yeah.

755
00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:35.360
I love that.

756
00:33:35.440 --> 00:33:40.800
I mean, that was a running thing for Michael as a character is that he would sometimes interrupt

757
00:33:40.800 --> 00:33:44.580
himself to have a great idea that were always terrible.

758
00:33:44.860 --> 00:33:46.100
Do you do that as a writer?

759
00:33:46.360 --> 00:33:50.880
Are you ever somewhere or at dinner, do you have a place where you put observations or ideas?

760
00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:53.140
Do you have a writer's notebook?

761
00:33:53.140 --> 00:33:58.120
I have scraps because I don't ever have the special writer's notebook with me.

762
00:33:58.220 --> 00:33:58.580
Of course.

763
00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:05.240
So, but I do have a box in my house and I'll tear, you know, I'll throw in the napkin or

764
00:34:05.240 --> 00:34:06.960
the back of the envelope.

765
00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:07.640
Yeah.

766
00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:09.020
And I'll throw it into the box.

767
00:34:09.240 --> 00:34:12.540
And then, so then you sit down, you have to write a new script or come up with a new

768
00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:12.900
character.

769
00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:17.920
Do you open the box and it's like, you look for the magical scrap that's going to be the

770
00:34:17.920 --> 00:34:18.240
seed?

771
00:34:18.240 --> 00:34:19.380
Pod over scraps.

772
00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:23.280
They're down to like really useless scraps at this point.

773
00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:25.180
I've used everything I possibly can.

774
00:34:26.200 --> 00:34:27.720
Well, why don't we take a break?

775
00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:33.920
Because when we come back, it's Michael's last bullpen scene ever.

776
00:34:34.640 --> 00:34:35.540
We'll be back.

777
00:34:44.650 --> 00:34:46.750
Well, we are back and here it is.

778
00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:49.650
This is Michael's last bullpen scene.

779
00:34:49.890 --> 00:34:53.570
This is the last thing we filmed with Steve Carell on The Office.

780
00:34:54.170 --> 00:34:56.130
He just walks out into the bullpen.

781
00:34:56.130 --> 00:35:02.210
He takes his unfinished mittens from Phyllis and he just stands there and looks at this

782
00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:03.230
ordinary workday.

783
00:35:03.750 --> 00:35:12.450
I remember going to the mix for this and cranking the sounds, the ordinary sounds of The Office.

784
00:35:12.610 --> 00:35:15.950
The phones ringing and the fax machines and stuff.

785
00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:17.230
Yeah, and we had tons of those.

786
00:35:17.410 --> 00:35:22.950
We had tracks for every type of like dot matrix printer, you know, over the years.

787
00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:26.870
And we just sort of like threw it all in, like, you know, jack it up.

788
00:35:26.990 --> 00:35:33.010
And there was this, I thought it was something where it was like an opportunity to just remind

789
00:35:33.010 --> 00:35:35.390
people of the sound of what it was.

790
00:35:35.530 --> 00:35:39.250
So to me, the sound was as important as the picture on that thing.

791
00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:45.130
You know, in the finale, you gave Pam that line about the beauty of ordinary things.

792
00:35:45.130 --> 00:35:45.610
Yeah.

793
00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:48.710
But I feel like that is the story of this episode as well.

794
00:35:48.930 --> 00:35:49.910
Yeah, it's in here for sure.

795
00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:50.830
Yeah, that theme.

796
00:35:51.090 --> 00:35:51.770
It's here.

797
00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:52.310
Yeah.

798
00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:57.670
Well, it's inherent in a documentary that you're looking at real people.

799
00:35:58.190 --> 00:35:58.530
Yeah.

800
00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:02.310
But that goes back to that poor guy who retired from SoCal gas.

801
00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:03.930
Yeah, exactly.

802
00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:04.750
Yeah.

803
00:36:04.970 --> 00:36:05.070
Yeah.

804
00:36:05.450 --> 00:36:06.350
Do you remember?

805
00:36:06.590 --> 00:36:11.470
Because I, so I was not in the scene, but I was there because after we shot this day,

